<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cenepa War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cenepa War was a short conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> fought in January and February 1995 over outposts in the upper Cenepa valley, in an undemarcated stretch of the Cordillera del Cóndor. Ecuadorian forces held most of their positions, and a ceasefire was agreed under the guarantors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio de Janeiro Protocol</a>. Negotiations led to the final border settlement of 1998.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cenepa-war.jpg" alt="Cenepa War" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The University of Texas at Austin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cenepa_river_basin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The dispute between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> went back to the first decades of the 19th century, when both countries emerged from the Spanish colonies. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuadorian-peruvian-war">Ecuadorian–Peruvian War</a> of 1941 the two signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio Protocol</a> in 1942, brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, the guarantors of the settlement. Demarcation halted in 1948, when President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-velasco-ibarra">José María Velasco Ibarra</a> declared the Protocol impossible to implement in the Cordillera del Cóndor, and in 1960 Ecuador declared the treaty null and void. A 78 km strip of the border remained undemarcated.</p><p>Tensions rose after the Pachacútec Incident of July 1991 over a Peruvian outpost that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> claimed lay on its side. From 1994 Ecuador installed outposts named Cueva de los Tayos, Base Sur and Tiwinza in the Cenepa basin on the eastern side of the range, territory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> also claimed. In late 1994 a meeting of battalion commanders at Base Sur failed to settle the matter, and both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> sent reinforcements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>During December the Ecuadorian Army deployed Special Forces, artillery, BM-21 rocket launchers and teams with man-portable surface-to-air missiles on the heights. On 26 January 1995 Ecuadorian Special Forces attacked Peruvian conscripts setting up a heliport behind their outposts and drove them off, and the shooting war began. Both countries ordered general mobilisation the next day, and around 140,000 men were mobilised in all. The first Peruvian ground assault came on 28 January, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopters</a> and ground attack aircraft.</p><p>Several explanations have been offered for the course of the fighting. Peruvian reinforcements and supplies had to be flown in by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> from Ciro Alegría base, more than 110 km to the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> committed Special Forces and Iwia detachments of tribal people such as the Shuar, while the Peruvian troops were at first mostly young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, and Ecuadorian artillery fired from the heights of the range.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Casualty figures vary widely: official estimates give 94 dead, while the human rights organisation ALDHU put the dead of both sides at around 500. Mediation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> opened the talks that ended in the Brasilia Presidential Act of 26 October 1998. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> accepted the validity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio Protocol</a>, and it received 1 km2 at Tiwinza as private property, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, for commemorative use. The border was formally demarcated on 13 May 1999, and the observer mission MOMEP ended on 17 June 1999, closing one of the longest territorial disputes in the Western Hemisphere.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cenepa War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
